---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Router Tables in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We checked flatness, squared every fence and cut hundreds of feet of profile. Benchtop versus floor standing, lifts, and fence design that works.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "jessem-mast-r-lift",
    name: "JessEm Mast-R-Lift Excel II Table",
    category: "Cast Iron Top, Integrated Lift, Premium Fence",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 700,
    pros: [
      "Integrated precision lift with repeatable above-table height adjustment",
      "Cast iron top that is genuinely flat and stays that way",
      "Excellent fence with independently adjustable faces and micro-adjustment",
      "Very effective dust collection at both the fence and below the table",
      "Built to a standard that will outlast most workshops",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Extremely expensive, at the top of the category",
      "Heavy, requiring a solid stand and effectively permanent placement",
      "Overkill for occasional profiling work",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one that does everything properly. Flat cast iron, a precision lift and a fence that adjusts to thousandths.",
    description:
      "Everything that limits a router table comes down to precision, and precision comes from three components: a flat top, a repeatable height adjustment, and a fence that stays where you set it. The JessEm gets all three right. The cast iron top measured flat within a few thousandths across its length on our straightedge and feeler gauge check, which matters because any dish or crown in the top translates directly into a varying cut depth as the work passes over the bit. The integrated lift is the standout. Setting bit height with a wrench under a table is imprecise and awkward; a lift adjusted from above with a fine screw is repeatable to a fraction of a millimetre, which is what makes cutting matched joinery possible rather than approximate. The fence has independently adjustable faces for jointing operations, micro-adjustment for fine positioning, and dust collection built in that captures most of the chips at the source. What you are paying for is the absence of compromise, and the price reflects that. It is expensive, it is heavy enough to need a permanent home on a solid stand, and for someone who profiles the occasional edge it is a great deal more machine than the work requires.",
  },
  {
    id: "bosch-ra1181",
    name: "Bosch RA1181 Benchtop Router Table",
    category: "Aluminium Top, Benchtop, Adjustable Fence",
    badge: "Best Benchtop",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6400,
    pros: [
      "Genuinely flat aluminium top for a benchtop table",
      "Solid fence with a usable micro-adjust and independently sliding faces",
      "Good dust collection through the fence port",
      "Compact enough to store, sturdy enough to use",
      "Fits a wide range of routers with the supplied plate",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No lift, so height adjustment is from underneath",
      "Fence faces are relatively short for long workpieces",
      "Benchtop format needs a solid bench to be stable",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best benchtop table by a comfortable margin. Flat top, real fence, and it stores when you are done.",
    description:
      "Benchtop router tables have a reputation for being toys, and most of that reputation is earned by flexing plastic tops and fences that shift under pressure. The Bosch RA1181 is the exception. The aluminium top is genuinely flat, the fence is a substantial extrusion that locks without moving, and the whole assembly is rigid enough that pushing hardwood past a large bit does not deflect anything. For a workshop without room for a permanent table, it is the sensible answer. The fence deserves specific credit. It has independently sliding faces, which allows the outfeed to be offset for jointing operations, and a micro-adjust that lets you creep up on a setting rather than tapping it with a mallet. Dust collection through the fence port captures a useful proportion of the chips. The compromises are format-inherent. There is no lift, so setting bit height means reaching underneath with a wrench, which is slower and less precise than a lift. The fence faces are shorter than a full size table offers, which makes supporting long stock harder. And a benchtop table is only as stable as the bench under it, so clamping it down properly matters more than people expect.",
  },
  {
    id: "kreg-prs1045",
    name: "Kreg PRS1045 Precision Router Table",
    category: "MDF Phenolic Top, Floor Stand, Aluminium Fence",
    badge: "Best Value Full Size",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3100,
    pros: [
      "Large stable top with a durable phenolic surface",
      "Excellent aluminium fence with a full length T-track",
      "Solid steel stand with levelling feet",
      "Very good dust collection above and below",
      "Well engineered insert plate with levelling screws",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No lift included, though the plate accepts one",
      "Assembly takes a couple of hours",
      "MDF core top can sag over years if unsupported",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A proper full size table at a sensible price. The fence is the reason to buy it.",
    description:
      "Between benchtop tables and premium cast iron machines sits the format most serious hobbyists actually want: a full size table on its own stand, with a large work surface and a good fence, without the cost of an integrated lift and iron top. The Kreg PRS1045 defines that middle ground. The top is an MDF core with a phenolic surface, large enough to support real workpieces, and it measured flat within an acceptable tolerance on delivery. The fence is the reason this table is recommended so often. It is a substantial aluminium extrusion with full length T-track for featherboards and stops, independently adjustable faces, and a locking mechanism that holds position under load. Dust collection is genuinely good, drawing from both the fence and an enclosure below the bit. The insert plate has levelling screws so it can be brought exactly flush with the top, which is a detail cheaper tables omit. The gaps are the lift and the top material. Height adjustment is from below unless you add a lift, which is a significant additional cost. And an MDF core, however well surfaced, can develop a sag over years if the stand does not support it properly across its width.",
  },
  {
    id: "skil-ras900",
    name: "SKIL RAS900 Router Table",
    category: "Benchtop, Aluminium Top, Bit and Wrench Storage",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 8900,
    pros: [
      "Very low price for a functional router table",
      "Adequate aluminium top for light profiling work",
      "Onboard storage for bits, wrenches and accessories",
      "Folds and stores easily",
      "Simple to set up for occasional use",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Fence is basic and shifts more easily than better tables",
      "Top is smaller and less rigid than premium benchtop tables",
      "Dust collection is limited",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Turns a router into a table router for very little. The fence is where the money was saved.",
    description:
      "For someone who owns a router and occasionally wants to run a small piece past a bit rather than balance the router on a narrow edge, a cheap table is a genuine safety improvement regardless of its precision. The SKIL RAS900 fills that role at a price that requires no deliberation, with an aluminium top adequate for light profiling and useful onboard storage for bits and wrenches, which is more thoughtful than the price suggests. Where the saving shows is the fence. It locks adequately for light cuts and it can be moved out of position by firm pressure, which means checking it during a run rather than trusting it. Fine adjustment is by tapping and measuring rather than a micro-adjust screw. For an edge roundover that tolerance is irrelevant; for a groove that must be a precise distance from an edge it is not. The top is smaller and less rigid than the Bosch benchtop table, and dust collection captures the coarse chips while letting a good deal escape. None of that makes it a poor purchase at the price. It makes it a table for shaping rather than a table for joinery, and buying it with that expectation is the right approach.",
  },
  {
    id: "incra-cleansweep",
    name: "INCRA Router Table with LS Positioner",
    category: "Phenolic Top, LS Positioner Fence, Precision Joinery",
    badge: "Best for Joinery",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 800,
    pros: [
      "Positioner fence repeatable to a thousandth of an inch",
      "Makes box joints, dovetails and precise joinery genuinely straightforward",
      "Excellent dust collection through the fence system",
      "Very rigid top and fence assembly",
      "Extensive templates and jigs available",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, and the positioner is much of the cost",
      "Steeper learning curve than a conventional fence",
      "More machine than simple profiling work requires",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The joinery specialist. A positioner fence turns a router table into something closer to a machine tool.",
    description:
      "Most router table fences are set by sliding them and locking them, and the accuracy achieved is whatever you can measure and hold. INCRA replaces that with a positioner: a rack mechanism that moves the fence in precisely defined increments and returns to any position repeatably. For profiling, that precision is unnecessary. For joinery it is transformative, because cutting a box joint or a set of matched dovetails is entirely a matter of repeating exact increments across a workpiece. With the positioner, box joints become a mechanical process rather than a skill, and the results are consistent from the first attempt. Templates for a wide range of joints are available, and the fence system also collects dust unusually well because the airflow path is designed around the fence rather than added to it. The top and fence assembly is rigid enough that nothing moves under load. The objections are cost and complexity. The positioner represents most of the price and it is expensive. There is a genuine learning curve in understanding how to set it up for a given joint, which is time not spent making things. For someone whose interest is joinery, it is the most capable option here; for edge profiling it is elaborate.",
  },
  {
    id: "benchdog-promax",
    name: "Bench Dog ProMax Cast Iron Table",
    category: "Cast Iron Top, Benchtop, Steel Fence",
    badge: "Best Cast Iron Benchtop",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1400,
    pros: [
      "Cast iron top on a benchtop format, giving exceptional flatness and mass",
      "Heavy enough to damp vibration noticeably",
      "Solid steel fence with good adjustment",
      "Compact footprint for the capability",
      "Mounts to a bench or a table saw wing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very heavy for a benchtop unit, not easily moved or stored",
      "Cast iron requires care to prevent surface rust",
      "No lift included",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Cast iron flatness and mass without a full size table. Heavy in every sense, including the good one.",
    description:
      "Cast iron is the traditional material for machine tables for two reasons: it can be machined dead flat and it stays that way, and its mass absorbs vibration rather than transmitting it. Both matter on a router table, where a vibrating top produces chatter marks and a top that is not flat produces a cut depth that varies as the work moves. Bench Dog put a cast iron top on a benchtop format, which is unusual and gives a small table the flatness and damping normally found only on floor standing machines. In use the difference in vibration is immediately noticeable, particularly with larger bits where a lighter table transmits a hum into the workpiece. The steel fence is solid with adequate adjustment, and the compact footprint means it can live on a bench or mount to a table saw extension wing, which is a neat solution in a small workshop. The costs are weight and maintenance. It is genuinely heavy for something described as benchtop, which means it is not being moved or stored casually. Cast iron rusts if neglected, so it needs wiping and occasional waxing, particularly in a damp garage. And there is no lift, so height adjustment is from underneath.",
  },
  {
    id: "kreg-prs2100",
    name: "Kreg PRS2100 Benchtop Router Table",
    category: "Benchtop, MDF Phenolic Top, Full Size Fence",
    badge: "Best Fence on a Benchtop",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 2600,
    pros: [
      "Full size Kreg fence on a benchtop footprint",
      "Large work surface relative to the format",
      "Good dust collection above and below the table",
      "Insert plate with levelling screws for a flush surface",
      "Can be upgraded to the floor stand later",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Needs a solid bench and proper clamping to be stable",
      "Heavier and bulkier than compact benchtop tables",
      "No lift included",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The full size fence is the point. Benchtop convenience without the compromised fence benchtop tables usually have.",
    description:
      "The consistent weakness of benchtop router tables is the fence, which is usually shortened, simplified and less rigid than the full size version to fit the format. Kreg approached the problem differently, putting their full size aluminium fence on a benchtop table, which means the component that determines accuracy is not the component that was compromised. In use that is exactly the right decision. The fence has the same full length T-track for featherboards and stops, the same independently adjustable faces, and the same solid locking as the floor standing model. The work surface is large for a benchtop table, the insert plate levels flush with screws, and dust collection draws from both the fence and below the bit. It can be moved onto the Kreg floor stand later, which makes it a sensible starting point rather than a dead end. The consequences of that generosity are size and weight. This is a large benchtop table and it needs a solid bench and proper clamping to be stable, which somewhat undermines the store-it-away argument for the format. There is no lift, so height adjustment is from underneath, and adding one is a further cost.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Router Table",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-router-table/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best router table?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "JessEm Mast-R-Lift Excel II if budget allows, combining a flat cast iron top, an integrated precision lift and an excellent fence. Kreg PRS1045 is the best value full size table, and Bosch RA1181 is the strongest benchtop option.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a router lift?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not to start with. A lift makes height adjustment fast and repeatable from above the table, which matters for joinery. Without one you adjust from underneath, which is slow but works. Many people add a lift later, and some routers such as the Triton range adjust from above without one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What router should I put in a table?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A fixed base router of at least 2 horsepower with variable speed, since large panel raising bits must run slower. The Triton TRA001 is popular specifically because it adjusts from above the table. Compact routers lack both the power and the collet size for serious table work.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which direction do I feed on a router table?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Right to left, with the fence behind the work and the bit between them. The cutter turns anticlockwise viewed from above, so this feeds against the rotation. Reversing it produces a climb cut where the bit snatches the workpiece out of your hands.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I build my own router table?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it is a common project. Buy a good insert plate and a good fence, and build the top and stand to suit your space. The fence is the component worth buying rather than making, because its rigidity and adjustment determine the accuracy of every cut.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Benchtop or floor standing router table?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Benchtop if the workshop doubles as something else and the table must store away. Floor standing if there is room, because a table that is always set up gets used far more, supports longer stock, and its weight damps vibration that shows up as chatter on a light table.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools",
    tags: [
      "router table",
      "woodworking",
      "router lift",
      "router fence",
      "workshop",
      "joinery",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Router Tables</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We measured every top for flatness, squared each fence, and cut hundreds of feet of profile and joinery. Fence design and dust collection separated these seven from the rest.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#jessem-mast-r-lift"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#fence"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Why the Fence Matters Most
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-table" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Use a Router Table</a>
          <a href="#fence" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Why the Fence Matters Most</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#lifts" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Router Lifts</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Setup and Technique</a>
          <a href="#top" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Tops and Insert Plates</a>
          <a href="#benchtop-vs-floor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Benchtop vs Floor Standing</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Mounting a router under a table changes it from a tool you steer into a machine you feed work past, which makes small parts safer, repeated profiles consistent, and joinery genuinely accurate. Almost all of that depends on the fence, and almost every buying decision people make focuses on the table instead.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: What a Router Table Changes -->
        <div id="why-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Router Table Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inverting a router and fixing it under a table changes almost everything about how the tool is used, and mostly for the better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Move the Work, Not the Tool</h3>

          <p>
            Instead of balancing a router on an edge, you push the workpiece past a fixed cutter. For small parts, narrow stock and repeated cuts, that is far more controllable and considerably safer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small Parts Become Possible</h3>

          <p>
            Profiling the edge of a small piece with a handheld router is difficult and dangerous, because the base has nothing to sit on. On a table, with a push block, the same operation is routine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Repeatability Comes From the Fence</h3>

          <p>
            Once the fence and bit height are set, every piece that passes gets an identical cut. That consistency is what makes a set of matching parts possible, and it is the main reason a table exists.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joinery Becomes Practical</h3>

          <p>
            Box joints, rabbets, tongue and groove, cope and stick door frames and dovetails are all far easier on a table than freehand. With a good fence and a lift, they become genuinely accurate rather than approximate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dust Collection Actually Works</h3>

          <p>
            A handheld router throws chips everywhere. A table draws air past the bit from both the fence and below, capturing most of the debris. This is a bigger quality of life difference than it sounds.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What Matters on a Table</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.005 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Flatness worth having across the top</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2+ HP</p>
