        <!-- SECTION 2: Why the Fence Matters Most -->
        <div id="fence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why the Fence Matters Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            People shop for router tables by looking at the top, and then spend the next several years fighting the fence. It is the wrong order.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The fence sets the cut, not the table.</strong> Depth of cut horizontally is determined entirely by where the fence sits relative to the bit. A fence that moves under pressure changes the cut mid-pass, which shows on the workpiece immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Independently adjustable faces enable jointing.</strong> Offsetting the outfeed face slightly forward lets a straight bit act as a jointer, taking a shaving off an edge and supporting the cut portion. Fences with a single fixed face cannot do this.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Micro-adjustment is the difference between setting and guessing.</strong> Moving a fence by a fraction of a millimetre by tapping it is imprecise and frustrating. A screw adjustment lets you creep up on a fit, which is how joinery gets tight.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>T-track carries featherboards and stops.</strong> Featherboards holding stock against the fence and down onto the table improve both safety and cut quality. Without track to mount them, they have to be clamped awkwardly or not used.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The fence should be the dust collection point.</strong> Most chips are thrown backwards into the fence, so a port there captures more than one below the table. Good tables collect from both.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Long faces support long work.</strong> Short fence faces let a long board pivot as it passes, which produces a curved cut. This is a real limitation of compact benchtop tables.
          </p>
        </div>

