        <!-- SECTION 4: Router Lifts and Height Adjustment -->
        <div id="lifts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Router Lifts and Height Adjustment
          </h2>

          <p>
            Setting bit height is the operation you perform most often on a router table, and how it is done determines whether the table is pleasant or infuriating.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Without a lift you adjust from underneath.</strong> That means crouching, reaching past the dust hose, releasing a lock, turning a knob you cannot see, and then checking the height from above. It works and it is slow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A lift raises the router from above the table.</strong> A screw mechanism accessed through the insert plate moves the router precisely, usually with a scale reading in thousandths. Setting a height becomes a ten second operation with a repeatable result.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Repeatability is what makes joinery work.</strong> Cutting a mortise and a matching tenon, or a cope and stick pair, requires returning to exact settings. A lift with a graduated dial does that; a router adjusted by feel does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Some routers have above-table adjustment built in.</strong> The Triton range in particular can be adjusted through the base plate with a supplied winder, which delivers most of the benefit of a lift for none of the cost. This is a legitimate way to avoid buying one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Lifts are expensive and often bought later.</strong> A quality lift costs as much as a mid-range router table. Many people buy the table, live with under-table adjustment, and add a lift once they know they need it, which is a reasonable sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check plate compatibility before buying either.</strong> Lifts and insert plates come in a few common sizes, and a plate that does not match your table opening means routing the opening or buying again.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Buy the Fence, Add the Lift Later</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If the budget forces a choice, spend it on the fence. A poor fence limits every cut you will ever make on the table and cannot be worked around. Under-table height adjustment is merely slow, and a lift can be added at any point once you know the table earns its space. Almost nobody regrets this order.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Setting Up and Using It Accurately -->
        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Setting Up and Using It Accurately
          </h2>

          <p>
            A well set up cheap table outperforms a badly set up expensive one, and setup is mostly a matter of a few checks people skip.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Level the insert plate to the top.</strong> A plate sitting proud or low creates a step the workpiece rides over, which changes the cut. Levelling screws exist for this and setting them with a straightedge takes five minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Square the fence to the table, not to the edge.</strong> The fence must be perpendicular to the table surface so the cut is square through the thickness. Check it with an engineer square rather than assuming the mounting is accurate.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Feed right to left, against the rotation.</strong> On a table the bit turns anticlockwise viewed from above, so the work moves from right to left with the fence behind it. Feeding the other way is a climb cut and the bit will snatch the work from your hands.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never trap the workpiece between the fence and the bit.</strong> Cutting with the fence on the far side of the bit from the work leaves nowhere for the piece to go and it will be thrown. The fence always supports the work on the near side.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use featherboards and push blocks.</strong> Holding stock against the fence and down onto the table with featherboards keeps the cut consistent and keeps your hands away from the cutter. On small parts, a push block is not optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Take multiple shallow passes.</strong> A profile cut in three passes is cleaner, safer and easier on the router than the same profile in one. Move the fence progressively rather than removing everything at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use starting pins for curved work.</strong> Routing a curved edge against a bearing without the fence requires a pin to brace the work as it enters the cut. Feeding a curve freehand into a spinning bit without one is how work gets thrown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Tops, Insert Plates and Dust -->
        <div id="top" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Tops, Insert Plates and Dust
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table surface does less work than the fence, but three things about it affect every cut and are worth checking before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Flatness determines consistency of cut.</strong> A dished or crowned top changes the distance from the surface to the bit as the workpiece travels, so a groove that starts at the right depth finishes at the wrong one. Check a top with a straightedge on arrival, because they are not all as flat as advertised.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cast iron is flattest, phenolic is most stable.</strong> Iron can be machined dead flat and adds mass that damps vibration, at the cost of weight and rust. A phenolic-surfaced MDF core is lighter, immune to rust, and can sag over years if the stand does not support it across its width.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The insert plate must sit exactly flush.</strong> A plate proud of the surface creates a step the workpiece climbs; one sitting low lets the piece dip. Levelling screws around the plate are the fix, and setting them with a straightedge takes five minutes and improves every cut afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Reducing rings close the gap around the bit.</strong> A large opening under a small bit leaves a hole for narrow work to drop into. A set of rings that fill the plate opening down to just larger than the cutter is a small thing that prevents ruined workpieces.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Collect dust from above and below.</strong> Most chips fly backwards into the fence, so a port there captures the majority. Enclosing the router below and drawing air through catches what falls, and also keeps the motor cooler and cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A miter slot is useful and not essential.</strong> It carries a miter gauge or a coping sled for end grain work. Many excellent tables omit it, and a simple shop-made sled running against the fence does most of the same jobs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Benchtop or Floor Standing -->
        <div id="benchtop-vs-floor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Benchtop or Floor Standing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Format is the first decision and it is mostly about workshop space rather than capability.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Benchtop tables store away.</strong> For a garage that is also a car park, that is decisive. The compromise is that they need a solid bench and proper clamping, and setting up each time adds friction that discourages use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Floor standing tables are always ready.</strong> A table on its own stand is set up, dust hose connected, fence squared, at any moment. In practice that means it gets used for jobs where dragging out a benchtop unit would not feel worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work support scales with the table.</strong> A large top supports long stock through the cut. On a small table, a long board is cantilevered and pivots, which produces an inaccurate cut regardless of the fence.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Table saw wing mounting is the space saving compromise.</strong> Mounting a router plate in an extension wing of a table saw gives a large flat surface and a fence, using space that already exists. It works well and it means the two machines cannot be used simultaneously.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Weight is stability.</strong> A heavy table absorbs vibration and does not move when you push work past a large bit. A light table on a light bench moves, and that movement appears in the cut as chatter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Build your own if space is unusual.</strong> A shop-made table using a bought insert plate and fence is a common and sensible project, and it lets the dimensions suit the space rather than the other way round.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best router table?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                JessEm Mast-R-Lift Excel II if budget allows, combining a flat cast iron top, an integrated precision lift and an excellent fence. Kreg PRS1045 is the best value full size table, and Bosch RA1181 is the strongest benchtop option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a router lift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to start with. A lift makes height adjustment fast and repeatable from above the table, which matters for joinery. Without one you adjust from underneath, which is slow but works. Many people add a lift later, and some routers such as the Triton range adjust from above without one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What router should I put in a table?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A fixed base router of at least 2 horsepower with variable speed, since large panel raising bits must run slower. The Triton TRA001 is popular specifically because it adjusts from above the table. Compact routers lack both the power and the collet size for serious table work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which direction do I feed on a router table?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Right to left, with the fence behind the work and the bit between them. The cutter turns anticlockwise viewed from above, so this feeds against the rotation. Reversing it produces a climb cut where the bit snatches the workpiece out of your hands.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I build my own router table?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is a common project. Buy a good insert plate and a good fence, and build the top and stand to suit your space. The fence is the component worth buying rather than making, because its rigidity and adjustment determine the accuracy of every cut.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Benchtop or floor standing router table?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Benchtop if the workshop doubles as something else and the table must store away. Floor standing if there is room, because a table that is always set up gets used far more, supports longer stock, and its weight damps vibration that shows up as chatter on a light table.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Router Table Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Router table errors range from inaccurate to dangerous, and the dangerous ones all involve feed direction.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feeding in the Wrong Direction</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The bit turns anticlockwise viewed from above, so work moves right to left with the fence behind it. Feeding left to right is a climb cut, and on a table the cutter will snatch the workpiece and throw it. This is the accident router tables are known for and it is entirely avoidable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trapping the Work Between Fence and Bit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the fence sits on the far side of the bit from the workpiece, the piece is trapped with nowhere to go and will be thrown. The fence always supports the work on the near side. This catches people out when they try to cut a groove in the middle of a wide board.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Table and Ignoring the Fence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The fence determines horizontal depth of cut, jointing capability, dust collection and whether featherboards can be mounted. A flat top with a poor fence limits every cut you will ever make, and unlike a lift, a bad fence cannot be easily worked around later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing Too Much in One Pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full profile cut in a single pass overloads the bit, burns the edge, strains the router and increases the chance of the workpiece being grabbed. Move the fence progressively and take three shallow passes; the result is cleaner and the whole operation is calmer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

